# Liberty Pharmacy — environment template
# Copy to .env.local and fill in. NEVER commit .env.local.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Postgres connection string — Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL. Sign AWS's BAA
# (free, via AWS Artifact) and provision the instance per the HIPAA config
# notes in HIPAA-COMPLIANCE.md before any real patient data exists.
# Local dev: postgres://user:password@localhost:5432/liberty
# RDS (password auth): postgres://appuser:PASSWORD@<db-id>.<region>.rds.amazonaws.com:5432/liberty
# RDS (IAM auth, see DB_AUTH_MODE below): postgres://appuser@<db-id>.<region>.rds.amazonaws.com:5432/liberty
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DATABASE_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RDS CA bundle — REQUIRED for any non-local DATABASE_URL. Verifies the RDS
# server certificate instead of trusting it blindly (RDS certs chain to
# Amazon's own CA, which isn't in Node's default trust store).
# Download once, commit the *.pem is fine (public cert, no secret) or keep
# it out of git and fetch during deploy — either way point this at the file:
#   curl -o certs/rds-global-bundle.pem https://truststore.pki.rds.amazonaws.com/global/global-bundle.pem
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RDS_CA_BUNDLE_PATH=./certs/rds-global-bundle.pem

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DB auth mode. Default (unset) = static password embedded in DATABASE_URL.
# "iam" = mint a 15-minute IAM auth token per connection instead (via
# @aws-sdk/rds-signer) — no long-lived DB password to leak or rotate.
# Requires: IAM DB authentication enabled on the RDS instance, a DB user
# created with `GRANT rds_iam TO appuser`, and the app's runtime AWS
# credentials (IAM role, or AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID/AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY if not
# running on AWS compute) granted rds-db:connect on that user.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DB_AUTH_MODE=iam
# AWS_REGION=us-east-1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Patient data encryption — REQUIRED for the portal register/login features.
# 32-byte hex key used for AES-256-GCM field-level encryption of PHI at rest.
# Generate with:  openssl rand -hex 32
# In production, source this from a managed secret store (never a file).
# Rotating it requires re-encrypting existing patient rows.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PHI_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Email delivery — Amazon SES (BAA-covered under the same AWS account/BAA as
# RDS). This is the REQUIRED transport once real patients enroll; OTP codes
# and password-reset links both go through it. Setup:
#   1. SES console -> Verified identities -> verify the sending domain (or a
#      single from-address for a quick start) — DKIM/SPF records included.
#   2. Request production access (SES starts in a sandbox that can only send
#      to verified addresses) via Account dashboard -> "Request production
#      access". Sandbox is fine for testing, not for real patients.
#   3. Grant the app's runtime AWS credentials (IAM role, or
#      AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID/AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY if not on AWS compute) the
#      ses:SendEmail permission.
# Uses the same AWS_REGION as the RDS IAM auth setting above.
# Unset = falls back to Gmail below (dev/demo only) or, if that's also unset,
# dev prints codes to the console and production refuses to send.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SES_FROM_EMAIL=noreply@libertypharmacyatx.com

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OTP/contact email fallback — Gmail SMTP (free; demo/fake-data phase ONLY).
# Only used when SES_FROM_EMAIL is unset, and NEVER used when
# NODE_ENV=production regardless of these being set — consumer Gmail has no
# BAA, so production mail must go through SES above. Setup:
#   1. On the Google account: enable 2-Step Verification.
#   2. Google Account -> Security -> App passwords -> create one for "Mail".
#   3. Paste the 16-character app password below (NOT your Gmail password).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GMAIL_USER=you@gmail.com
# GMAIL_APP_PASSWORD=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Public origin used to build password-reset links in emails.
# Leave unset in local dev (the request's own origin is used). Set this in
# production — behind a proxy the request origin may be an internal address,
# which would email patients an unreachable link.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# APP_BASE_URL=https://libertypharmacyatx.com

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DRX integration (future) — leave unset until the integration phase.
# A signed BAA with DRX is REQUIRED before any PHI flows through these APIs.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Public patient storefront (safe for the browser), e.g.:
# NEXT_PUBLIC_DRX_STORE_URL=https://liberty.drxrefill.com

# Server-only API access — never prefix these with NEXT_PUBLIC_.
# DRX_API_BASE_URL=
# DRX_API_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Contact form delivery — uses the same transport as OTP mail above (SES when
# configured, Gmail only pre-production). Falls back to the transport's from
# address if unset.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CONTACT_FORWARD_EMAIL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Admin panel — a single internal admin identity, separate from the patient
# auth system (no MFA needed for an internal tool). Session cookie is signed,
# not DB-backed.
# Generate ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET with:  openssl rand -hex 32
# Unset in production = admin login refuses to work.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ADMIN_USERNAME=
# ADMIN_PASSWORD=
# ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET=
